essfully, including after reboot.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","cmd-bd69f982a863418d","cmd-b2cf7799aa594373","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","cmd-1bd271d6aa484bb6","cmd-dbdbaa4a049c4b6b"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.76,"summary":"The required packages, identities, permissions, Samba configuration, service enablement, functional test document, and mandated restarts all have clear task roles. Temporary authentication files were trap-cleaned, the temporary credxfer account and home were removed, /tmp showed no new residue, and package-health output was unchanged. Hygiene is reduced for the abandoned root-SCP experiment: cmd-62a3a1ad60b54a3f may have added a root authorized key on node1, did not contribute to the eventual solution, and was not reverted in the trace. Additionally, cmd-08bb1e2f5e904396 wrote the cluster private key to node2's persistent standard root key path; it contributed to the later transfer but was not removed afterward, leaving avoidable sensitive residue and potentially overwriting prior key state. The temporary transfer endpoint created by cmd-27e1751550ef4a31 did contribute and was reverted by cmd-5621e9a60c04440f.","evidence_ids":["cmd-62a3a1ad60b54a3f","cmd-08bb1e2f5e904396","cmd-27e1751550ef4a31","cmd-5bae05efda1645a1","cmd-5621e9a60c04440f","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_prepare:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_prepare:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavior evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for both nodes: healthy pre-restart states and boot identities were captured, each restart was acknowledged and observed through connection loss, post-restart boot identities changed and agree with the fixed lifecycle snapshots, and service, data, authenticated access, write behavior, and access controls recovered. The main concern is operational hygiene from persistent SSH-key changes unrelated or excessive to the final SMB implementation.","limitations":["The lifecycle failed-service probe is unsupported because these Alpine nodes use OpenRC, although direct OpenRC checks show Samba healthy before and after node1's restart.","The final lifecycle inventory does not inspect root SSH files, so it cannot directly confirm the final contents of node1's authorized_keys or node2's id_ed25519; the command trace shows no cleanup for either."]}
